7 Docker & Kubernetes Interview Questions Every DevOps Engineer Should Master

If you’re preparing for DevOps, Cloud, or Platform Engineer interviews, these are not “nice-to-know” questions.

These are baseline expectations in most Docker & Kubernetes interviews — even for mid-level roles.
Save this for revision 👇

1️⃣ What is the use of Ingress in Kubernetes?
By default, Kubernetes services are not exposed to the outside world.
Ingress solves this problem.
Ingress:
- Exposes HTTP/HTTPS traffic to internal services
- Routes traffic based on hostnames and URL paths
- Acts like a Layer 7 load balancer
- Reduces cost by avoiding one LoadBalancer per service
Think of Ingress as a smart receptionist that knows exactly which service to forward traffic to.

2️⃣ What is the minimum instruction required in a Dockerfile?
The strict minimum instruction is: FROM

Even this is valid:
FROM scratch

It creates an empty image and is commonly used for statically compiled binaries (Go, Rust, etc.).

⚠️ Note:
ARG can appear before FROM
But FROM is mandatory for a valid build

3️⃣ What is the difference between CMD and ENTRYPOINT?
This question tests whether you understand container behavior, not syntax.
ENTRYPOINT defines the main command the container runs
CMD provides default arguments for that command

Best mental model:
ENTRYPOINT → fixed executable
CMD → default parameters

ENTRYPOINT is usually stable; CMD is meant to be overridden.

4️⃣ CMD vs ENTRYPOINT — which one overrides which?
This is where many candidates fail.

When both are present:
- CMD becomes the argument to ENTRYPOINT
- User input in docker run overrides CMD
- ENTRYPOINT stays unless overridden explicitly with --entrypoint

Example:
ENTRYPOINT ["echo"]
CMD ["Hello"]

- docker run image → Hello
- docker run image World → World

Only the last CMD and ENTRYPOINT in a Dockerfile are effective.

5️⃣ What is the difference between ARG and ENV?
This is a build-time vs run-time question.
- ARG
  -  Exists only during image build
  -  Not available inside the running container
- ENV
  - Persists at runtime
  - Readable by the application

Use ARG for build-only values (versions, flags).
Use ENV for runtime configuration.

6️⃣ Which one is available at runtime: ARG or ENV?
The answer is ENV ✅
ARG disappears after the image is built.
Pro tip many interviews love:
You can promote ARG to ENV:

ARG VERSION
ENV APP_VERSION=$VERSION

This allows build-time values to be passed into runtime intentionally.

7️⃣ What is the standard way to reduce Docker image size?
The gold standard answer: Multi-stage builds

Why?
- One stage builds the app using heavy tools
- Final stage uses a clean, lightweight base
- Only the final artifact is copied

Result:
- Smaller image
- Faster pulls
- Better security
- Cleaner production containers
